\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Complex Spin in dimension three is #{SL_2} on the spin module}

\p{Let #{V} be a three-dimensional complex quadratic space with a
supplied nondegenerate polarization

##{V=\mathbb C w\oplus\mathbb C z\oplus\mathbb C w',\qquad
B_Q(w,w')=1,\quad Q(z)=1,}

and choose the line coordinate so that #{\rho(z)=\alpha}, exterior
parity. On #{S=\bigwedge(\mathbb Cw)}, use the ordered basis
#{(1,w)}. The even Clifford action then gives a homomorphism

##{\Spin(Q)\longrightarrow SL(S)\simeq SL_2(\mathbb C).}

Reversal is matrix adjugation in this basis, so reverse norm one forces
determinant one. This is the determinant calculation behind the
dimension-three case; the target is the complex group, rather than the
compact real unit-quaternion group of \ref{fcap-002H}.}

\p{There is a concrete inverse. For #{c\in\mathbb C}, the two products

##{(z+cw)z,\qquad z(z+cw')}

are products of two norm-one vectors and hence are actual Spin elements.
Creation, contraction, and parity from \ref{fcap-002W} give their matrices

##{I+cE_{21},\qquad I+cE_{12}}

in the ordered basis #{(1,w)}. These elementary transvections generate
#{SL_2(\mathbb C)}. Writing a target matrix as a word in them and replacing
each factor by its displayed lift gives a preimage; faithfulness of the
even Clifford action makes the result independent of the chosen word.
Thus restriction and this lift are inverse group maps, and the same basis
intertwines the spin action with the standard two-dimensional module.}

\p{TauCeti proves this exact group-and-module statement over any field
with #{2\ne0}, given polarization data and dimension three
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.exists_spinGroup_mulEquiv_specialLinearGroup_and_spinRep_equiv_stdSLRep_of_finrank_eq_three}.
Its chosen finite basis may reindex the two exterior coordinates; the
formulas above use the stated basis #{(1,w)} and the corresponding basis
change. The theorem needs no additional separable-closure hypothesis once
the polarization is supplied.}

\p{Fulton--Harris identify #{\Spin_3(\mathbb C)} with
#{SL_2(\mathbb C)} and distinguish it from the compact real form
#{SU(2)}
\citet{Section 10.3, pp. 143--144; Exercise 20.39, p. 311}{fulton2004representation}.
Goodman--Wallach independently list the same complex exceptional
isomorphism
\citet{Section 6.3.3, Exercise 1, p. 322}{goodman2009symmetry}.
Their statements name the target; the two Clifford products above supply
the coordinate lifts used here. Meinrenken's #{\Spin(3)=SU(2)} is instead
the compact real comparison
\citet{Section 3.7.6, p. 83}{meinrenken2013clifford}.}
